    faqs: [
        { q: "Do I need a permit for Tsomgo Lake?", a: "Yes — because Tsomgo lies in a sensitive border zone, every visitor needs a Protected Area Permit, including Indian citizens. Foreign nationals face additional restrictions and cannot always proceed to the lake or on to Nathula. Permits are arranged a day in advance through registered operators, which we handle for our guests." },
        { q: "How high is Tsomgo Lake, and is altitude a concern?", a: "The lake sits at around 3,753 metres, so the thin air affects many visitors. We keep the day gentle, advise on acclimatising in Gangtok first, and plan realistically; guests with heart or breathing conditions should tell us in advance so we can adapt." },
        { q: "Can I stay overnight at the lake?", a: "No. Tsomgo has no accommodation and lies in a restricted high-altitude border zone, so it is always visited as a day trip from Gangtok, about 2–2.5 hours away by mountain road. We build it as a permitted day excursion." },
        { q: "When does Tsomgo Lake freeze?", a: "The lake typically freezes over in the depths of winter, roughly December to February, when the whole basin is under snow — spectacular but very cold and dependent on the road remaining open. Spring brings blooms on the banks, and autumn brings clear reflections of the peaks." },
        { q: "Can I continue to Nathula Pass from Tsomgo?", a: "Sometimes. Eligible Indian visitors can, with an additional permit and if conditions allow, continue up the old Silk Route to the Nathula Pass on the China border. Foreign nationals are generally not permitted to Nathula. We advise honestly on what the day's permits and weather allow." },
    ],
